 ## Overview
-The NDC Ray Transformation maps camera-space rays into a canonical bounding cube $[-1, 1]^3$ to enable uniform sampling in disparity (inverse depth) for forward-facing, unbounded scenes [S1][S3, S4, S5, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray generation which assumes bounded metric depth, this method shifts ray origins to the near plane and projects them such that linear sampling in the transformed space corresponds to linear sampling in disparity from the near plane to infinity [S1][S3, S4, unofficial]. It is primarily used for real-world captures (e.g., LLFF) where the scene extends to infinity, whereas standard ray generation is reserved for synthetic bounded scenes (e.g., Blender) [S6, unofficial].
+The NDC Ray Transformation maps camera-space rays into a canonical bounding cube $[-1, 1]^3$ to enable uniform sampling in disparity (inverse depth) for forward-facing, unbounded scenes [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial][S5, unofficial]. Unlike standard ray generation which assumes bounded metric depth, this method shifts ray origins to the near plane and projects them such that linear sampling in the transformed space corresponds to linear sampling in disparity from the near plane to infinity [S1][S3, unofficial][S4, unofficial]. It is primarily used for real-world captures (e.g., LLFF) where the scene extends to infinity, whereas standard ray generation is reserved for synthetic bounded scenes (e.g., Blender) [S6, unofficial].
 
